WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.125
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:10.251
I've always wondered, why in a nation so

00:00:10.251 --> 00:00:13.920
rich are some people so poor?

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.970
To me it's always seemed so old fashioned.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.040
Like governments that don't
resemble a democracy or

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.545
voting rights that don't include women or
like these wool cardigans.

00:00:27.545 --> 00:00:31.255
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Things that just shouldn't exist.

00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:35.140
[LAUGH]
Growing up,

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.830
I often asked myself this question.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.930
Why does poverty still exist?

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.050
As a daycare provider,
my mother made enough money for

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.060
us to live just above the poverty line.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.730
Despite providing a critical service
to society, she stressed every day

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.330
about whether we'd have enough to pay for
rent or buy groceries.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.770
She'd work more and
more hours just to escape being

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.338
poor as if it were
a privilege she had to earn.

00:01:06.338 --> 00:01:09.583
To me, that just didn't seem right.

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.479
The truth about poverty and

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:16.860
inequality in the US is far worse than
my own childhood might have indicated.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.480
In the United States, one out of every
five children live in absolute poverty.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.410
A poverty rate higher than what prevails
in virtually all other rich nations.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.014
Inequality in the US is
the highest it's been since 1928,

00:01:32.014 --> 00:01:34.278
right before the Great Depression.

00:01:36.486 --> 00:01:41.238
Over the past 35 years,
the real average income of

00:01:41.238 --> 00:01:46.100
the top 0.01% has grown
by 322% while that of

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.760
the bottom 90% has largely
remained stagnant.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.150
And the American dream of working hard and

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.710
finding a way out of
poverty no longer applies.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:07.280
Increasingly, the greatest predictor
of your future success Is not

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:15.520
your education or your perseverance but
your parents' income and your race.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.800
To make matters worse economists
now say that inequality is so

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.369
large that it's had a negative
impact on overall economic growth.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.256
Something we can all care about.

00:02:32.131 --> 00:02:36.718
People are poor absolutely,
relatively, and

00:02:36.718 --> 00:02:42.230
permanently, and
all at a cost to our nation's growth.

00:02:44.994 --> 00:02:45.720
So what can we do?

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:47.940
About two years ago,

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.210
after reading everything I could
find to understand poverty and

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:55.330
inequality better and what we can do to
address it, I stumbled across a book.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.080
It's called Utopia for
Realists by Rutger Bregman.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.420
In the book, he chronicles an idea, one so

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.120
simple I thought it
couldn't possibly work.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:13.410
It's an idea that dates back 500 years
to Thomas Moore in his own book,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.180
called Utopia.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.990
Some refer to it as the citizen's
dividend, others call it a basic income.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:23.820
So what is it?

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:30.280
It's an unconditional monthly cash grant,
enough to pay for your basic needs.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.680
Food, shelter, education, that's it.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.417
It's not a favor, but a right.

00:03:41.338 --> 00:03:45.132
As divisive as it might seem it's
actually an idea supported by

00:03:45.132 --> 00:03:50.074
individuals across the political spectrum,
from civil rights activists, Dr.

00:03:50.074 --> 00:03:55.158
Martin Luther King Jr., who praised it for
its fairness to free market economist,

00:03:55.158 --> 00:03:59.550
Milton Friedman, who appreciated
how it instilled freedom of choice.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.060
Now when I tell people that we should
just give free money to everyone,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.900
they usually say, You socialist!

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.240
And then they respond with
one of three concerns.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.490
People will spend it on drugs and alcohol.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.820
People will stop working.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.990
We can't afford to pay for it.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.360
So today, I hope to share what I learned
about each of these three concerns, and

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.470
hopefully show that a basic income is more
than just an idea, it's a proven idea.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.530
Number one,
people will spend it on drugs and alcohol.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.910
The underlying assumption
with this concern,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:48.150
stated bluntly, is that there is somehow
something wrong with poor people.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.990
Poor people make poor decisions,
like doing drugs and alcohol,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.849
and that's probably why
they're poor to begin with.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.370
Because of this mindset,
the approach has always been the same.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.890
If we could just teach them,

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.780
if we could teach them how to live
life with our values and our culture.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.230
If they would only just listen.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.290
What this mindset fails to recognize are
the drastic effects of living in poverty.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.120
Some poor people make poor
decisions because they're poor.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.950
However, if you change the context,
you can change the actions too.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.700
World Bank researchers found that in
19 different cash transfer programs

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.500
across ten different nations, spending
on alcohol and cigarettes went down.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.140
Given hope and a brighter future,
individuals stopped using temptation goods

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.690
as a way to cope with
their current situation.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:52.440
Instead, the extra cash went to pay for
their kids' school fees, to buy medicine,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.080
to repair their homes,
to invest in their small businesses,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.900
to further grow their wealth.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.990
As Bregman concluded,
poverty is not the result

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:10.280
of poor decision making or
lack of moral character.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.635
Poverty is a lack of cash.

00:06:12.635 --> 00:06:19.157
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Number two, people will stop working.

00:06:20.929 --> 00:06:25.180
In the 1970s there's a basic income
experiment in a small town in Canada.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:30.810
Every family received enough money
to ensure they were lift above

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.270
the poverty line.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.529
And for four years all went well,
until a new Canadian government was voted

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:39.515
into power that immediately
ended the experiment.

00:06:42.043 --> 00:06:47.140
25 years later, a Canadian professor
found and analyzed these records.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.295
And the results were clear.

00:06:50.651 --> 00:06:55.133
School performance went up,
while mental health complaints,

00:06:55.133 --> 00:07:00.120
domestic violence incidents and
hospitalization rate all went down.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.860
And no one quit their jobs.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:10.517
The only ones who worked a little
less were new mothers caring for

00:07:10.517 --> 00:07:14.896
their child, and students,
who decided to stay in school longer.

00:07:17.783 --> 00:07:19.960
Which brings me to the third and
final concern.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.810
Even if this is all true,
we can't afford to do it.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:30.370
Actually, we can't afford not to do it.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.180
Poverty is extremely expensive.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.840
The cost of child poverty in the US
is estimated at $500 billion.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:45.030
In terms of higher healthcare costs,
higher drop out rates, and more crime.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:50.190
This is an incredible
waste of human potential.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.720
A basic income could be financed
with what's known as a negative

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:56.640
income tax credit.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.990
Which essentially means that your income
is pumped up as soon as you fall below

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:01.630
the poverty line.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:08.780
Under this scenario,
according to economists best estimates,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:13.923
for a cost of $175 billion,
less than 1% of GDP, less than

00:08:13.923 --> 00:08:20.137
4% of our nation's annual budget,
we could actually eradicate poverty.

00:08:23.137 --> 00:08:26.660
As Bregman noted basic income is
like venture capital for the people.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.820
We invest now and
after 10 to 20 years it'll pay for itself.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.820
In terms of lower health care cost,
less crime, and

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.139
more productive citizens putting the money
to work and stimulating our economy.

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:47.550
According to current estimates, a basic
income started today would result in

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:52.948
an increase of GDP of 13% by 2025, or

00:08:52.948 --> 00:08:58.223
$2.5 trillion.

00:08:58.223 --> 00:09:02.240
So when given cash,
people spend less on drugs and

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.670
alcohol, work just as much, and
we actually save and generate money.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.780
Well if all this is so obviously
true why haven't we done it already?

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.470
The truth is we tried.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.112
In the late 1960's we
piloted a basic income for

00:09:19.112 --> 00:09:23.763
close to 9,000 individuals
across seven different states.

00:09:25.804 --> 00:09:29.692
And by every measure,
as you might have guessed by now,

00:09:29.692 --> 00:09:32.600
the pilot was a success, except for one.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:38.807
Researchers found that for
those families receiving a basic income,

00:09:38.807 --> 00:09:41.192
divorce rates increased by 50%.

00:09:43.104 --> 00:09:47.260
Now, this was something politicians of
the time just could not get behind.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.420
A basic income had apparently
given women too much independence.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.647
However, just ten years later
in a reanalysis of the data,

00:09:57.647 --> 00:10:01.620
researchers found that this
was a statistical error.

00:10:04.268 --> 00:10:07.660
But by that time,
the political moment had passed.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.530
Now we are seeing a re-emergence
of the basic income.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:17.560
Silicon Valley has taken up the idea based
on fear that robots will one day take

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.220
our jobs.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:23.560
However, while the future is uncertain,
the present is clear.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.540
Poverty exists where and
when it shouldn't.

00:10:30.926 --> 00:10:34.730
We need to rid ourselves of
the biases we have about the poor.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.580
We need to acknowledge the wealth,
we have to address the problem and

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.420
the potential that we
will unlock if we do so.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:52.250
We need to redefine our social contract
to say that no one will live in poverty.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.760
But, to do this,
most of all we need the will.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:05.070
And that will must be founded on
the belief that a life without poverty

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:09.530
should not be a privilege
you have to earn but

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:14.314
a right that we all deserve.

00:11:14.314 --> 00:11:17.878
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:17.878 --> 00:11:21.407
[MUSIC]

